{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/149818-top-heavy-mummy-culture","id":149818,"title":"Top Heavy Mummy Culture","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-05-26T17:04:07-07:00","id":149818,"title":"Top Heavy Mummy Culture","content":"Apologies if this has already been discussed at some point. I just started reading Cursed Necropolis D.C. and found the numbers of Guildmasters to be quite striking. 7 out of the 24 Arisen in the City are Guildmaster status, close to 30% of the population. Not only that, but there are many Arisen in the city who believe Washington's size warrants a large number, 10 Guildmasters instead of 7, which would take the number of Status 5 individuals in the city to over 40% of the population. On top of that, there are 17 Arisen remaining after the 7 Guildmasters are taken into account. That's not enough for even one member of each rank in each Guild.<br /> <br /> So this got me thinking. I don't think Guildhalls of the Deathless actually addressed this matter in all that much detail, but clearly Mummy Society is top-heavy. And this sort of makes sense. Mummies were craftsmen who generally rank their status along an Apprentice to Journeyman to Master-type relationship. After 6,000 years, the only people likely still to be considered Apprentice-rank (Status 1-2) in their respective craft are those who have been demoted back to that level due to some infraction, or else the rare Arisen leaving their Guild for a new one. The overwhelming majority of Arisen are going to fall into the Status 3-4 range as a consequence, with Status 5 Arisen clearly making up a not-inconsiderable portion of the population themselves.<br /> <br /> I plan to start running a Mummy campaign sometime in August, and I've been working on building the city. At the moment I have 9 Arisen, not counting the 3-4 player characters. One player and two NPCs are the city's Guildmasters, which seems about right going by the numbers above. I might actually up the Guild Status of one or two of the existing NPCs, making the low-ranking Arisen all the more scarce.<br /> <br /> Anyway, that's how it appears to me. Anyone agree/disagree/see anything I might have missed?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-26T18:32:24-07:00","id":149892,"title":"","content":"It's worth remembering that 1) DC is unusual in its prestige and importance and 2) there are apparently an abnormally large number of transient Arisen of all guilds floating in and out of DC, and these guildmasters may be serving for members in other nomes, such as Baltimore and Richmond, who do not merit a guildmaster in that particular nome due to a lack in numbers. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2096-willywonka","name":"willywonka","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"45"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-26T20:45:17-07:00","id":149997,"title":"","content":"I don't know how much one's skill in the guild's craft is relevant to guild status. It seems apparent that it would, but, at the very least, the Arisen's mortal lives were a long, long time ago. The guilds - as organized by Mummies, rather than how they were as mortals in the Empire - didn't really come about until the first Sothic turn. They modeled them after what they could remember from their previous lives, but it's not a perfect correlation","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-26T22:27:14-07:00","id":150041,"title":"","content":"I don't necessarily think that the skill in the Guild's craft is remotely relevant, exactly. Mummies can't make relics anymore, after all, so a big part of their duties in life are no longer possible to carry out. What I mean is more in terms of the Guild mentality, and what the terms &quot;Apprentice&quot;, &quot;Journeyman&quot;, and &quot;Guildmaster&quot; say about the people that devised them and how they think about their place in the social structure. Try convincing someone that after 6,000 years of loyal service that they should still be considered an Apprentice. If your social structure is rooted in a craftsman's terminology like that, even if the actual duties pertaining to each &quot;rank&quot; are no longer relevant, anyone with a measure of pride is going to bristle at being considered an Apprentice. Especially considering that the difference in experience after so many centuries is marginal at most.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-27T06:59:52-07:00","id":150289,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Azahul</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/149818-top-heavy-mummy-culture?p=150041#post150041\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't necessarily think that the skill in the Guild's craft is remotely relevant, exactly. Mummies can't make relics anymore, after all, so a big part of their duties in life are no longer possible to carry out. What I mean is more in terms of the Guild mentality, and what the terms &quot;Apprentice&quot;, &quot;Journeyman&quot;, and &quot;Guildmaster&quot; say about the people that devised them and how they think about their place in the social structure. Try convincing someone that after 6,000 years of loyal service that they should still be considered an Apprentice. If your social structure is rooted in a craftsman's terminology like that, even if the actual duties pertaining to each &quot;rank&quot; are no longer relevant, anyone with a measure of pride is going to bristle at being considered an Apprentice. Especially considering that the difference in experience after so many centuries is marginal at most.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually the difference in experience depends greatly on a number of factors, key among which would be 'how often have I been woken up by my cultists to go around doing things?' There's not a single answer there.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Revlid wrote:<br /> Yes, hollowing out your humanity to become an utterly utilitarian asura is the exact suggestion I would expect from you, Aiden.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/168-aiden","name":"Aiden","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar168_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-27T08:46:46-07:00","id":150382,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aiden</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/149818-top-heavy-mummy-culture?p=150289#post150289\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Actually the difference in experience depends greatly on a number of factors, key among which would be 'how often have I been woken up by my cultists to go around doing things?' There's not a single answer there.</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally, I feel &quot;how high is my memory&quot; to be the key here (or possibly &quot;have I recorded the experience?&quot;). However many times you've been woken up is irrelevant, in terms of experience, if you can't remember the events to learn from them. And DC is full of Mummies of high rank with low Memory. After a certain amount of time, experience is much of a muchness, and what I've read of Mummy society thus far hasn't made much of a deal in correlating experience with Guild Status.<br /> <br /> Regardless, it's been 6,000 years. Even the least active Mummy has no doubt lived for decades of that period. And with the heavy skew towards high-ranking Mummies, it really does suggest that the lower ranks are filled with either Mummies that don't care too much about their Status, or are there due to exceptional circumstances.<br /> <br /> In all honesty, has anyone seen any evidence anywhere of a pyramid-like Mummy social structure (as in large numbers of low-ranking Mummies, few high-ranking ones)? Because it seems more like a rectangle, maybe even on the verge of becoming an inverted pyramid, from what I've read.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-28T10:36:06-07:00","id":151449,"title":"","content":"The way I tend to play it is that Status is a (mostly) static modifier independent of Memory (though sometimes you could remember you used to be higher or low and have been changed over time) attached to your accomplishments in life. The Shan'iatu chose the best and brightest tools, but they built strong foundations for something of a pyramid should there ever be a need for a hierarchy (or rather, nomarchy) for their purposes in the future. Being a guildmaster means you were a guildmaster in Irem, and were one of the greatest at your craft, though now even that is (theoretically) lost to you.<br /> <br /> Because of the nature of the Descent, it's such that the power structures sort of change even though the Status seems locked in place. I could see that nomarchs and guildmasters tend to be tied to places, while journeymen Arisen tend to be transient, and apprentices perhaps mostly tied to merets or their cults.<br /> <br /> Guildhalls didn't. for me, really resolve or make interesting the idea of a 6,000 year old society of Deathless immortals, and I always felt the nome structure was, as you said, top-heavy and hard to reconcile with the supposed rarity of the Arisen. I think that personally, DC's writeup helps solidify the idea that nomes tend to be sort of points where nomarchs and their merets and other hangers-ons are stable, and Arisen society is not really geographically tied down in most of the same way for similar reasons as Promethean (especially considering Sybaris). This fits pretty well with the pulp feel they intended to go for.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3058-juvenaliansatyr","name":"JuvenalianSatyr","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3058_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-28T14:45:37-07:00","id":151699,"title":"","content":"It might hep to expand your concept of Arisen society beyond the actual Arisen. In most games, we've become conditioned to think of each splat as its own independent society (though from what I've read, the Idigam Chronicles are looking to change that with Wolf-Blooded pack members. In this game, it's less about an Arisen society, than it is about an Irem-in-Exile, with the Arisen playing the role of the new Shaniatu (flawed as they may be, although Seb-Hetchet seems to have concluded that the Arisen have managed a fair copy on their own). Arisen may be the most prominent &quot;face&quot; of their respective cults, these include cultists of the mortal, ghost, sadikh, revenant, and intruder varieties. In some cases (Sedge), we even see that these individuals receive equal consideration within the context of their loyalty and skills.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2096-willywonka","name":"willywonka","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"45"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-28T16:25:05-07:00","id":151811,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>willywonka</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/149818-top-heavy-mummy-culture?p=151699#post151699\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It might hep to expand your concept of Arisen society beyond the actual Arisen. In most games, we've become conditioned to think of each splat as its own independent society (though from what I've read, the Idigam Chronicles are looking to change that with Wolf-Blooded pack members. In this game, it's less about an Arisen society, than it is about an Irem-in-Exile, with the Arisen playing the role of the new Shaniatu (flawed as they may be, although Seb-Hetchet seems to have concluded that the Arisen have managed a fair copy on their own). Arisen may be the most prominent &quot;face&quot; of their respective cults, these include cultists of the mortal, ghost, sadikh, revenant, and intruder varieties. In some cases (Sedge), we even see that these individuals receive equal consideration within the context of their loyalty and skills.</div> </div> </div> </div>Absolutely on that last front, but the question was specifically about the Guilds and their structure, which are essentially Arisen-only, and the nomarch system is explicitly tied to Guildmasters as I remember it. For me, Arisen society is a lot more about pyramids and layers of conspiracies intersecting on several levels on a global scale that isn't usually explored in World of Darkness, interplayed with very personal stories (the Descent). All the actual &quot;society&quot; is really layers and intersections of cults that are tied together. Look at any &quot;map&quot; of Freemason descent or Illuminati &quot;sub-groups&quot; and you really get a nice idea of what it looks like, but I've always like injecting the conspiracy theory elements in my game so that's more my take than the basic thrust given i nthe books.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3058-juvenaliansatyr","name":"JuvenalianSatyr","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3058_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"18"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-31T04:31:15-07:00","id":154692,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JuvenalianSatyr</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/149818-top-heavy-mummy-culture?p=151449#post151449\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The way I tend to play it is that Status is a (mostly) static modifier independent of Memory (though sometimes you could remember you used to be higher or low and have been changed over time) attached to your accomplishments in life. The Shan'iatu chose the best and brightest tools, but they built strong foundations for something of a pyramid should there ever be a need for a hierarchy (or rather, nomarchy) for their purposes in the future. Being a guildmaster means you were a guildmaster in Irem, and were one of the greatest at your craft, though now even that is (theoretically) lost to you.</div> </div> </div> </div>While I concur that status doesn't shift for Mummies anywhere near as often as it does for other kinds of supernatural, I haven't really got this vibe from the books I've read. It does shift, even if over the course of centuries. There are a number of characters in D.C. who weren't Guildmasters in life, and either are or (in Sedge's case) intent on becoming them. And Guildhalls has quite a bit of material describing the ceremonies a few Guilds use to elevate their members to new ranks or cast them down.<br /> <br /> Personally, I actually find myself increasingly warming to the idea of Mummy society being largely concentrated around status 3-5. It's a nice change of pace from Changeling, which I've been playing a lot of lately, and its fairly standard Court structure. Most Mummies operate fairly independently from their guilds anyway, and that's an attitude wholly in character for a journeyman craftsman.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}